\section{Gradient Analysis of the SSA Method}

One of the main contributions of SSA is its ability to align sparse attention with full attention. To encourage this alignment while maintaining sparsity, we introduce an $L_1$-based regularization term. Although Lasso is traditionally used to induce sparsity in learned feature spaces, here the regularization is applied directly in the attention-distribution space. This makes it important to analyze the gradient behavior of the proposed loss and understand its effect on sparsity.

We define the alignment losses as:
\begin{equation}
L_{\text{sparsity}} = \|a_{\text{full}} - \mathrm{sg}[a_{\text{sparse}}]\|_1,
\qquad
L_{\text{commitment}} = \|a_{\text{sparse}} - \mathrm{sg}[a_{\text{full}}]\|_1,
\end{equation}
where $\mathrm{sg}[a_{\text{sparse}}] = \nabla a_{\text{sparse}}\big|_{t_x}$ denotes the gradient of the sparse 
attention with respect to the next decoding token $t_x$. 
Without loss of generality, we focus on $L_{\text{sparsity}}$.

Because $L_{\text{sparsity}}$ is an $L_1$ loss, its gradient with respect to $a_{\text{full}}$ takes the standard 
subgradient form:
\begin{equation}
\nabla L_{\text{sparsity}} =
\begin{cases}
1,  & a_{\text{full}} > \mathrm{sg}[a_{\text{sparse}}],\\[2mm]
0,  & a_{\text{full}} = \mathrm{sg}[a_{\text{sparse}}],\\[2mm]
-1, & a_{\text{full}} < \mathrm{sg}[a_{\text{sparse}}].
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
A crucial observation is that $\mathrm{sg}[a_{\text{sparse}}]$ may be negative. 
This corresponds to sparse attention signaling that a token is important for predicting the next decoding token:
increasing attention to this token would reduce the loss. 
In contrast, a positive value indicates an unimportant token.

However, since attention weights satisfy $a_{\text{full}} \ge 0$, the condition 
$a_{\text{full}} < \mathrm{sg}[a_{\text{sparse}}]$ can never occur when $\mathrm{sg}[a_{\text{sparse}}] < 0$. 
Therefore, the case $\nabla L_{\text{sparsity}} = -1$ is impossible for important tokens. 
As a result, important tokens always induce a gradient of $+1$, which weakens the corresponding full-attention 
value instead of strengthening it.

For unimportant tokens where $\mathrm{sg}[a_{\text{sparse}}] > 0$ and $a_{\text{sparse}} \approx 0$, the loss behaves 
like a standard Lasso penalty, pushing $a_{\text{full}}$ toward zero.

For unimportant tokens where $\mathrm{sg}[a_{\text{sparse}}] > 0$ and $a_{\text{sparse}} \approx 0$, the loss behaves 
like a standard Lasso penalty, pushing $a_{\text{full}}$ toward zero.

In summary, although this loss superficially resembles a Lasso term, its behavior differs because 
$\mathrm{sg}[a_{\text{sparse}}]$ may take negative values. 
This leads to two key effects:
\begin{itemize}
    \item It promotes sparsity in both full and sparse attention distributions.
    \item It prevents unbounded reinforcement when both attention mechanisms identify the same important token. 
          Without this bounded effect, both attentions could repeatedly strengthen the same token, resulting in 
          gradient explosion.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Discussion of Alternative Implementations}
We next examine how alternative alignment regularizers influence gradient behavior.

\noindent\textbf{$L_2$-based regularization.}
Using an $L_2$ alignment term yields:
\begin{equation}
L_{\text{sparsity}} = \|a_{\text{full}} - \mathrm{sg}[a_{\text{sparse}}]\|_2^2,
\end{equation}
with gradient
\begin{equation}
\nabla L_{\text{sparsity}}
= 2\big(a_{\text{full}} - \nabla a_{\text{sparse}}|_{t_x}\big)\,\nabla a_{\text{full}}.
\end{equation}

Here, the update magnitude scales linearly with the difference 
$(a_{\text{full}} - \nabla a_{\text{sparse}}|_{t_x})$. 
If sparse attention marks a token as important (i.e., $\nabla a_{\text{sparse}}|_{t_x} < 0$), this difference becomes 
large and positive, amplifying the gradient on $a_{\text{full}}$. 
Such multiplicative amplification accumulates across layers and can grow almost exponentially, leading to unstable 
updates or gradient explosion.

\noindent\textbf{KL divergence--based regularization.}
If KL divergence is used:
\begin{equation}
L_{\text{sparsity}} = \mathrm{KL}(a_{\text{full}} \,\|\, \mathrm{sg}[a_{\text{sparse}}]),
\end{equation}
the gradient becomes:
\begin{equation}
\nabla L_{\text{sparsity}}
= -\frac{\nabla a_{\text{sparse}}|_{t_x}}{a_{\text{full}}|_{t_x}}\;\nabla a_{\text{full}}.
\end{equation}

The division by $a_{\text{full}}|_{t_x}$ strongly amplifies updates when full-attention values are small, which is common in sparse distributions. 
When sparse attention identifies an important token ($\nabla a_{\text{sparse}}|_{t_x}<0$), this ratio becomes highly 
positive, again producing large gradient magnification. 
Reversing the KL direction yields the same issue.

Thus, both the $L_2$ and KL alignment objectives inherently encourage aggressive mutual reinforcement of important tokens, creating the risk of gradient explosion. The $L_1$ formulation used in SSA avoids this behavior by providing uniformly bounded gradients.

